  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 221 PM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nHigh pressure over the southeast is expected to spin in place \nthrough the rest of the work week and into the weekend. As the days \ncontinue to grow shorter and the leaves drop, we'll need to be \nmindful of the combination of dry fuels and low relative humidity as \nwe being our fall Fire Season.\n\nTemperatures during the daytime will climb into the low to mid 70s \nacross most of the CWA before we see some low 80s before the weekend \nas the winds swing around to the south. As that high pressure begins \nto break down, we'll see the chances of showers and possibly a \nthunderstorm start to increase late on Saturday evening before \nsweeping through on Sunday. While the threat of severe weather is \nmarginal, the models do show a decent amount of rain on Sunday with \nvalues ranging from 1.5-2.5\". Current drought conditions should \nminimize the overall flood threat, though some localized flash \nflooding will be possible with training storms and in the metro \narea. \n\nThe system will clear out late Sunday, giving us a few more days of \nclear fall weather before the models start to disagree on what's \ncoming next.",
